WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.540
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:11.360
Today we are going to take these old low-quality
photos, and ask an AI what it thinks they

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:12.830
should look like.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:19.630
And yes, we are going to see a restored version
of this photo of Einstein, and this one too.

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:22.280
And the results are absolutely amazing.

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:28.820
In fact, I could hardly believe some of the
results so I had to check them over and over

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:29.820
again.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.660
So, what is the problem here?

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:38.040
Well, we have one input photo, which is a
blurry mess, and when we try to restore it

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.840
with a previous method, the output is…also
a blurry mess.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:47.390
This was DFDNet, a research paper from 2020.

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:51.510
Surely, something better must be out there
by now.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.780
And, yes there is!

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.690
This is GFP-GAN, a technique from 2021.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:03.960
This is significantly better, but the hair
is full of artifacts, and the photo itself

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.140
is still not that sharp.

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.760
Now, have a look at this.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:15.611
This is GPEN, also a paper from 2021, and
this is much better than its predecessors.

00:01:15.611 --> 00:01:23.460
But if you think that this image has detail,
now have a look at the new technique.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.040
Whoa, this is so much better.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:32.460
The hair has tons of detail, the glasses finally
look right, and now that we are comparing

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:36.860
the two side by side, we see an interesting
effect.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:43.750
The previous method created an idealized image
where a lot of wrinkles and other high-frequency

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:46.020
effects are now gone.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:51.079
And believe it or not, this aspect of the
new AI is controllable.

00:01:51.079 --> 00:01:53.950
I’ll tell you about it in a moment.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:02:00.860
Now, this new technique is so good, we can
even use it on old photos, and get this, even

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:02.180
movies.

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:07.840
Due to copyright issues, I can only show you
still images from here, but I made sure to

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.819
put the full videos in the video description
so you Fellow Scholars can have a look.

00:02:12.819 --> 00:02:18.460
I will note that I have found some temporal
coherence issues, in other words, some jumpy

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:21.580
behavior, but it was very mild.

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:27.720
I think many people wouldn’t even notice
that an AI restoration algorithm was at work

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:28.720
here.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.730
And I know what you are waiting for.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:32.750
Now, it’s Einstein time.

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:38.690
I will say in advance that we are going to
talk about what this new AI thinks Einstein

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:39.690
looked like.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:41.330
We cannot be for sure.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:45.810
Now hold on to your papers and let’s see.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:46.940
Whoa.

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:53.060
This is incredible image restoration, and
the technique delivers amazingly well over

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:54.170
and over again.

00:02:54.170 --> 00:03:00.750
Now, once again, we cannot say that this is
exactly what Einstein looked like, but I feel

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:04.130
like with every paper, we get closer and closer.

00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:10.030
And here, I will try my best to show you the
strengths and weaknesses of this algorithm

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:12.060
so you can decide for yourself.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:17.530
Now, I promised that we are going to talk
about identity preservation.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.730
So let’s have a look together at this.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:26.860
We can use this parameter before restoration,
this preserves the identity of the subject,

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:29.959
at the cost of some additional quality.

00:03:29.959 --> 00:03:36.180
Or, if we are looking for a super-high quality
output, and are okay with the results not

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:40.590
looking like exactly the same person, we can
also do this.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:47.420
Yes, this gives us a super-high quality image,
but the magnitude of the changes can be such

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:49.659
that we get a different person.

00:03:49.659 --> 00:03:54.400
Now, my first thought was that well, what
is the point of this?

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.120
Why would anyone use this?

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:59.120
But then it struck me.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:01.890
Are you thinking what I am thinking?

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:02.890
Look!

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:03.890
Got it?

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:08.519
We can use this on synthetic characters created
by an AI.

00:04:08.519 --> 00:04:15.349
These are sometimes highly imperfect images,
and in this case, we can supposedly get a

00:04:15.349 --> 00:04:17.810
super high quality version of this human.

00:04:17.810 --> 00:04:19.070
Let’s see!

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:23.699
Wow, now that’s what I call a huge difference.

00:04:23.699 --> 00:04:29.070
Restoration happened, the facial features
are a little different, but this is a synthetic

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:34.570
character, in this case, we might decide that
we don’t mind this tradeoff so much.

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:40.780
Now, for the case when we pull the slider
to the right to have slightly lower quality,

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:44.870
but preserve the identity of the image better.

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:47.479
This is obviously super useful.

00:04:47.479 --> 00:04:52.009
However, how do we know if these results are
truly good?

00:04:52.009 --> 00:04:57.710
We cannot compare this with high-fidelity
photos of Einstein, the whole point of this

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:01.500
technique is that such high-quality photos
don’t exist.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:07.740
Well, one way is to take an image of someone
we know, delete parts of it, have it restore

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.570
it, and compare to the original.

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:16.700
Now, there will be some failure cases from
previous techniques that look a tiny bit frightening,

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:20.240
so if you are not ready for that, stop the
video now.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:22.430
Otherwise, let’s see.

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:23.430
Whoa.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:30.010
To say that some of these previous methods
were not so good at this would be an understatement.

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:33.389
Now let’s see the new technique.

00:05:33.389 --> 00:05:34.389
Wow!

00:05:34.389 --> 00:05:39.750
This is so far beyond the previous techniques,
it is hard to believe that such progress can

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:42.130
be made in just one paper.

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:48.350
But, if we take the time to look through the
results, it delivers over and over again.

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:53.340
And at this point, there is no doubt in my
mind that we are going to see people who are

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:57.840
not with us anymore in all their glory on
our screens.

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:59.100
That is incredible.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:02.990
So, how long do we have to wait for such an
image?

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:05.169
Well, not long at all.

00:06:05.169 --> 00:06:11.580
This new technique can pull this off not in
minutes, and not even in seconds.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:15.140
It does this 14 times every second.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:16.140
Wow.

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:17.770
But you know what?

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:23.160
I have good news - you Fellow Scholars can
try it right now online!

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:25.090
Let the experiments begin!

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:27.050
So, what do you think?

00:06:27.050 --> 00:06:29.490
Whose image would you like to restore?

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:30.930
Isaac Asimov anyone?

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:33.169
Let me know in the comments below!

00:06:33.169 --> 00:06:50.520
Thanks for watching and for 

00:06:50.520 --> 00:07:30.919
your generous support, and I'll see you next
time!

